need to fix the mentions feature:
The SDK call chain
1. TimelineProxy.sendMessage() builds the message content and calls the Rust Matrix SDK — TimelineProxy.swift:400-409:


let messageContent = buildMessageContentFor(message, html: html,
    intentionalMentions: intentionalMentions.toRustMentions())

_ = try await timeline.send(msg: messageContent)          // normal send
// or
try await timeline.sendReply(msg: messageContent, eventId: inReplyToEventID)  // reply
2. buildMessageContentFor builds the Matrix event content, then attaches mentions via — TimelineProxy.swift:550-572:


// picks the right content type: plain markdown or formatted HTML
if let html {
    content = messageEventContentFromHtml(body: message, htmlBody: html)
} else {
    content = messageEventContentFromMarkdown(md: message)
}

return content.withMentions(mentions: intentionalMentions)  // ← attaches mentions
3. intentionalMentions.toRustMentions() converts to the Rust SDK type — IntentionalMentions.swift:


func toRustMentions() -> Mentions {
    Mentions(userIds: Array(userIDs), room: atRoom)
}
What this means for React Native
The Matrix JS SDK has the exact same concept. When sending a message you pass intentional_mentions alongside the content:


await room.client.sendMessage(roomId, {
  msgtype: 'm.text',
  body: 'Hey @John can you help?',
  format: 'org.matrix.custom.html',           // optional, if you have HTML
  formatted_body: 'Hey <a href="https://matrix.to/#/@john:server.org">John</a> can you help?',
  'm.mentions': {
    user_ids: ['@john:server.org'],
    room: false,                              // true for @room
  }
});
The m.mentions field (MSC3952) is what withMentions(mentions:) adds on the iOS side. It's a standard Matrix spec field — the SDK just provides a typed wrapper around it. In the JS SDK you set it directly on the event content object.

